% ---------------- Q1 ----------------
\section{Q1) User types and the features relevant to each (Version 1)}

Version 1 solves the three stated problems: nobody knows who has had a temperature taken, doctors visit before temperatures exist, and discharges are late. Everything else is deferred.

\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{2.6cm} Y Y@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{User type} & \textbf{Goal} & \textbf{V1 features and actions} \\
\midrule
Nurse &
Record each patient's temperature once a day, quickly. &
See the list of patients whose temperature is still due today. Record a temperature (\degC). Correct a mistaken reading from the same day. See critical readings (40.0\,\degC{} or higher) flagged. \\
\addlinespace
Doctor &
Visit every patient once a day after the temperature exists, and decide who can go home. &
See patients to visit, with today's temperature and the 3-day fever-free count. Mark a visit as done (only after a temperature is recorded). Recommend discharge once the cure criterion is met. Record a death. \\
\addlinespace
Admin staff &
Free beds on time and admit new patients. &
See the discharge queue. Approve discharge, which frees the bed. Admit a new patient into a free bed. See bed occupancy, temperatures and visits still due. \\
\addlinespace
Head doctor (read-only view in V1, uses the Admin screen) &
Know whether the centre meets the 85\% success benchmark. &
See success rate against 85\% and the mortality rate against the expected 15\%, with an alert when mortality rises above 15\%. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}

\medskip
\textbf{Deliberately out of V1 (nice to have):} treatment/medication records, patient login or family access, notifications by SMS, per-doctor workloads, trend charts, multi-site support.

% ---------------- Q2 ----------------
\section{Q2) Functional prototype}

\textbf{Working prototype link:} \url{https://claude.ai/artifact/UbHu4V1VUKP2PtQD4q2hyx}

The same single-file app can be deployed to Vercel or any static host without a build step. Data in the prototype is demo data held in the browser.

\subsection*{Screens and flow}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Role switch (top right):} Nurse, Doctor or Admin. Each role shows a different default list and different actions.
  \item \textbf{Header strip on every screen:} beds occupied (74/74), temperatures done today, doctor visits done today, patients awaiting discharge, plus the treatment success rate against the 85\% benchmark.
  \item \textbf{Nurse screen:} tab ``Temperature due'' lists patients with no reading today. Type a value and press Record. The row moves to ``Recorded today''. ``Correct reading'' replaces the same-day value.
  \item \textbf{Doctor screen:} tab ``To visit today''. Mark visited is disabled until the nurse has recorded a temperature. Recommend discharge works only after today's visit and 3 consecutive fever-free days. Record death asks for confirmation.
  \item \textbf{Admin screen:} tab ``Discharge queue'' with Approve discharge. The Admit patient panel adds a patient when a bed is free. ``Start next day'' is a demo control that moves the clock forward so the 3-day rule can be shown.
\end{itemize}

\subsection*{Suggested demo path}
\begin{itemize}
  \item As Nurse, record a temperature for a patient in ``Temperature due''. Try 45 to see the validation error.
  \item As Doctor, open ``Cure criterion met'', press Mark visited, then Recommend discharge.
  \item As Admin, open ``Discharge queue'' and Approve discharge. A bed is freed. Admit a new patient into it.
  \item Try Admit while all 74 beds are full to see the capacity block.
\end{itemize}

% ---------------- Q3 ----------------
\section{Q3) Logical constraints for developers}

These rules are not visible on the screens and must be enforced on the server as well as in the UI.

\subsection{A. Roles and permissions}

\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}Y c c c@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Action} & \textbf{Nurse} & \textbf{Doctor} & \textbf{Admin} \\
\midrule
Record or correct today's temperature & Yes & No  & No  \\
Mark doctor visit                     & No  & Yes & No  \\
Recommend discharge                   & No  & Yes & No  \\
Approve discharge (frees bed)         & No  & No  & Yes \\
Record death                          & No  & Yes & No  \\
Admit patient                         & No  & No  & Yes \\
View success and mortality rate       & No  & Yes & Yes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}

\medskip
Unauthorised actions are hidden or disabled in the UI and rejected by the API with a 403.

\subsection{B. Temperature rules}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Fever is 38.0\,\degC{} or higher. Store in \degC{} with one decimal place.
  \item Accept only 34.0 to 42.0\,\degC. Outside that range show ``Enter a temperature between 34.0 and 42.0\,\degC'' and do not save.
  \item One official reading per patient per calendar day. A second entry is treated as a correction and needs the nurse to press Replace. Keep the old value and the editor's name in an audit log.
  \item A reading of 40.0\,\degC{} or higher shows a ``critical'' tag and moves the patient to the top of the doctor's list.
  \item No future-dated readings, and no readings for discharged or deceased patients.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{C. Doctor visit rules}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Mark visited is blocked until today's temperature exists. Show: ``Temperature must be recorded by a nurse before the doctor visit.''
  \item One visit flag per patient per day. The flag resets at the start of each day.
  \item Patients with critical or rising temperatures are listed first.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{D. Discharge rules}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A patient is cured after 3 consecutive daily readings, each below 38.0\,\degC. A missing day breaks the streak.
  \item Recommend discharge is allowed only if: status is Admitted, the doctor visited today, today's reading is below 38.0\,\degC, and the streak is 3 or more. Otherwise show the specific reason (not visited, fever today, or ``Needs 3 consecutive fever-free days. Currently N.'').
  \item Approve discharge (admin) is allowed only for recommended patients. If a fever is recorded after the recommendation, the recommendation is withdrawn automatically, the nurse sees a message and the doctor is notified.
  \item Approval frees the bed immediately and counts the patient as a success. The decision cannot be reversed from the UI.
  \item Discharge is based solely on the 3-day criterion, as set by the head doctor.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{E. Capacity and admission}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Maximum 74 beds. If all are occupied, Admit is blocked with ``All 74 beds occupied. Discharge a patient first.''
  \item Assign the lowest free bed number. Never assign one bed to two patients (use a database constraint).
  \item A new patient starts with no readings. Their first temperature is due on the day of admission.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{F. Death and outcome tracking}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Record death needs an explicit confirmation step and frees the bed.
  \item Success rate $= \dfrac{\text{discharged}}{\text{discharged} + \text{deceased}}$. Benchmark 85\%. When mortality is above 15\%, show the red alert to doctors and admins.
  \item Show rates only when at least one closed case exists, to avoid dividing by zero.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{G. Warnings and errors}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Show ``Start next day'' warning with the count of patients missing a temperature, so admins know before the day closes.
  \item Every error says what is wrong and what to do next. Never lose typed data when an error appears.
  \item Daily end-of-day report: patients with no temperature, patients not visited, patients with 4 or more fever-free days still not discharged.
  \item All writes are audit-logged with user, role and time.
\end{itemize}

% ---------------- Q4 ----------------
\section{Q4) Sprint planning}

\textbf{Capacity.} Velocity is 25 points. Already committed: $3 \text{ stories} \times 5 \text{ points} = 15$. Remaining capacity: 10 points.

\textbf{Value model.} Low $= 1$, Medium $= 3$ (3$\times$ low), High $= 6$ (2$\times$ medium).

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}c l c c r@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Priority} & \textbf{Value} & \textbf{Effort} & \textbf{Value / effort} \\
\midrule
A & High   & 6 & 1   & 6.00  \\
B & High   & 6 & 3   & 2.00  \\
C & High   & 6 & 5   & 1.20  \\
D & Medium & 3 & 2   & 1.50  \\
E & Medium & 3 & 3   & 1.00  \\
F & Medium & 3 & 1   & 3.00  \\
G & Low    & 1 & 0.4 & 2.50  \\
H & Low    & 1 & 0.5 & 2.00  \\
I & Low    & 1 & 0.3 & 3.33  \\
J & Low    & 1 & 0.1 & 10.00 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\textbf{Method.} This is a knapsack with capacity 10. Taking everything would need 16.3 points of effort (value 31), so 6.3 points must be dropped at the lowest possible value loss. Dropping C (effort 5, value 6) is the cheapest large cut, and one more cut of at least 1.3 effort is needed: D or E (effort 2 or 3, value 3 each). Any other combination loses at least 10 value.

\textbf{Answer:} add A, B, D, F, G, H, I and J. Do not add C or E.
\begin{itemize}
  \item Total added effort: $1 + 3 + 2 + 1 + 0.4 + 0.5 + 0.3 + 0.1 = 8.3$ points (within the 10 available).
  \item Total added value: $6 + 6 + 3 + 3 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 22$, the maximum achievable.
  \item Swapping D for E also reaches value 22 but costs 9.3 effort, so D is chosen to keep effort lower.
  \item Sprint load becomes $15 + 8.3 = 23.3$ points, leaving about 1.7 points of buffer.
\end{itemize}

